  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1+{t}^{2}$ and $q(t)=-5{t}^{2}+2t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9 & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2)(k-7)+(1)(k)+(2)(k-3) \\\\  & =2k-14+k+2k-6 \\\\  & =5k-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9=0$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 5k-20 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =20 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
